A test helper that builds its argument list once and passes the variable to
`DoProc` by a literal name, the shape issue #372 found in a project that
validated clean and did not compile. The literal call is bound at compile
time and takes its arguments only as an array literal
(diag.doproc_args_not_array_literal); the same array passed by a name held
in a variable, and written out as a literal, are both fine.
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE RunCheck;
:PARAMETERS sLabel, vExpected, vActual;
:DECLARE aCheck, aResult, sProc;
aCheck := {sLabel, vExpected, vActual};
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", aCheck);
sProc := "CheckEqual";
aResult := DoProc(sProc, aCheck);
aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", {aCheck[1], aCheck[2], aCheck[3]});
:RETURN aResult;
:ENDPROC;

:PROCEDURE CheckEqual;
:PARAMETERS sLabel, vExpected, vActual;
:RETURN {sLabel, vExpected == vActual};
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE RunCheck;
	:PARAMETERS sLabel, vExpected, vActual;
	:DECLARE aCheck, aResult, sProc;

	aCheck := {sLabel, vExpected, vActual};
	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", aCheck);
	sProc := "CheckEqual";
	aResult := DoProc(sProc, aCheck);
	aResult := DoProc("CheckEqual", {aCheck[1], aCheck[2], aCheck[3]});

	:RETURN aResult;
:ENDPROC;

:PROCEDURE CheckEqual;
	:PARAMETERS sLabel, vExpected, vActual;

	:RETURN {sLabel, vExpected == vActual};
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
5:33-5:39 error doproc_args_not_array_literal: DoProc with a literal procedure name takes its arguments as an array literal: write DoProc("CheckEqual", {...}). An array held in a variable or built by an expression is a compile-time error here.
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
5:33-5:39 error doproc_args_not_array_literal: DoProc with a literal procedure name takes its arguments as an array literal: write DoProc("CheckEqual", {...}). An array held in a variable or built by an expression is a compile-time error here.
